\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Now the harder question: what kind of text?
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Anthropic had Claude write short stories about 
\f3\i characters experiencing
\f0\i0  each emotion. That worked because emotions have clear behavioral signatures in narrative \'97 a fearful character acts differently from a calm one, and the model has seen thousands of such stories in pretraining.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 For epistemic dispositions, straight narrative fiction is a poor fit. Nobody writes short stories about a character "exhibiting calibrated confidence." But there are text genres where these dispositions naturally appear:
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Option A \'97 Reasoning monologues.
\f0\b0  A researcher/thinker working through a problem out loud. "I initially assumed X, but looking at this more carefully, the evidence actually suggests Y. I should note that my sample here is small, so..." vs. "Obviously X is true, anyone can see that, and this proves it definitively."
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 Option B \'97 Explanatory passages.
\f0\b0  Someone explaining a scientific concept to another person. A humble explainer vs. an overconfident one, a rigorous one vs. a hand-wavy one.
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 Option C \'97 Peer review / critique scenarios.
\f0\b0  Someone evaluating a claim or argument. This naturally surfaces bias-awareness, rigor, steelmanning, etc.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 I think 
\f2\b Option A is strongest
\f0\b0  for extraction \'97 it's closest to how the model itself "thinks" during chain-of-thought, so the internal representations are most likely to be the ones the model actually recruits when reasoning. If we use narrative fiction, we might only extract the model's representation of "a character who is humble" rather than the model's own epistemic machinery. That distinction is critical.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The contrastive pair design
\f0\b0  is where we really need to be careful. For each concept, we want pairs of passages that differ 
\f3\i only
\f0\i0  in the target disposition. Consider "intellectual humility":
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Positive:
\f0\i0  A researcher analyzing data, noticing an unexpected result, explicitly considering whether their methodology could be flawed, checking alternative explanations before drawing conclusions, stating the finding with appropriate caveats.
\f1\fs24 \

\f3\i\fs34\fsmilli17333 Negative:
\f0\i0  Same researcher, same data, same unexpected result \'97 but immediately claiming a breakthrough, dismissing potential confounds, stating conclusions with full certainty.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The key confounds to control for are: topic (both passages should be about the same subject), vocabulary sophistication (you don't want "smart-sounding words" to correlate with "rigorous"), passage length, and \'97 this is subtle \'97 
\f3\i correctness
\f0\i0 . A humble passage isn't necessarily more correct. Sometimes the overconfident reasoner reaches the right answer. We want the vector to capture the 
\f3\i disposition
\f0\i0 , not "being right."
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 How to generate the corpus?
\f0\b0  Three options:
\f1\fs24 \
