\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use Gemma 4 itself
\f0\b0  (or Claude via API) to generate the passages with careful prompting. Advantage: fast, scalable. Risk: the model might produce stereotyped versions of these dispositions \'97 "humble" might just mean "adds 'I could be wrong' at the end."\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Curate from real text.
\f0\b0  Pull passages from actual scientific papers, peer reviews, blog posts, textbooks. Advantage: authentic. Risk: enormous confounds (different topics, authors, styles), and very labor-intensive to find matched pairs.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Hybrid
\f0\b0  \'97 generate with an LLM but with very specific structural constraints, then manually review and filter. This is probably the right answer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf0 For the hybrid approach, the prompting strategy matters a lot. Rather than "write a passage demonstrating intellectual humility," you'd want something like: "Here is a scenario: [specific technical problem]. Write two versions of a researcher's internal reasoning about this. In version A, the researcher genuinely considers they might be wrong and explicitly traces their uncertainty. In version B, the researcher reasons with unjustified certainty." Then you review the pairs and throw out any where the contrast is shallow or relies on surface markers.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 How many passages per concept?
\f0\b0  Anthropic used one story per emotion, but they had 171 emotions. With ~20 concepts, we probably want 15-30 contrastive pairs per concept, spanning different topics and domains (physics, biology, economics, software debugging, etc.), so the vectors generalize across subject matter rather than encoding "talks about physics."
