\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 What you're essentially describing is a three-stage pipeline:
\f1\b0\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 Stage 1 \'97 Factual scaffolds.
\f0\b0  Curate real scientific scenarios \'97 actual discoveries, experimental results, reasoning chains \'97 stripped of identifying names and dates but keeping the core factual structure intact. These become "scenario skeletons." For example: "A researcher observes that a particular protein concentration correlates with disease severity across 47 patients. However, a subset of 8 patients shows the opposite trend. The initial hypothesis was..."
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 Stage 2 \'97 Generate first version.
\f0\b0  Feed the scaffold to an LLM with instructions to write it as a reasoning monologue, randomly assigned to either the positive or negative pole of the target concept. So this scaffold might get assigned "overconfident reasoning" for this particular pair.
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 Stage 3 \'97 Generate the contrastive twin.
\f0\b0  Feed the 
\f3\i completed
\f0\i0  first version back with instructions to produce the opposite pole while changing 
\f3\i as little as possible
\f0\i0 . This is the key insight you're raising \'97 by conditioning the second passage on the first, you get much tighter control over what actually differs between them.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 This is significantly better than generating both independently because independent generation introduces drift \'97 different sentence structures, different emphasis on different facts, different narrative flow. All of that becomes noise in your vector extraction. The "generate twin from original" approach maximizes the signal-to-noise ratio of what actually changes.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Now the concerns worth working through:
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Your point about the model "knowing it's fictional" when you anonymize real discoveries is real but I think overblown. The model doesn't need to be fooled. What matters is that the 
\f3\i activations during processing
\f0\i0  reflect the target disposition. Even if Gemma 4 internally recognizes "this is about CRISPR with names removed," the 
\f3\i reasoning style
\f0\i0  in the passage will still activate different representations depending on whether the narrator is being humble or overconfident. Anthropic's emotion stories were obviously synthetic too \'97 the model wasn't fooled into thinking these were real accounts of people feeling desperate. The vectors still worked.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 What I think is the real value of using factual scaffolds
\f0\b0  isn't about fooling the model \'97 it's about grounding the passages in real epistemic complexity. If you let an LLM freely generate a "scenario where a scientist reasons carefully," it'll produce toy problems. Real scientific scenarios have genuine ambiguity, messy data, plausible competing hypotheses. That messiness is exactly where epistemic virtues 
\f3\i matter
\f0\i0  and where the model's representations of them are most likely to be recruited.
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 On the guidelines.md \'97 absolutely yes.
\f0\b0  This becomes the reproducibility backbone of the whole project. What should go in it:
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The concept definitions \'97 not dictionary definitions but operational ones. "Intellectual humility" isn't just "being modest." For our purposes it means: explicitly acknowledging uncertainty, considering alternative explanations before committing, distinguishing strength of evidence from strength of conviction, being willing to revise. Each concept needs this level of specificity because the LLM generating the passages needs to know 
\f3\i what behaviors to produce
\f0\i0 , not just what label to invoke.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 Passage constraints \'97 target length (I'd say 200-400 tokens, long enough for the disposition to manifest across multiple reasoning moves, short enough to keep the signal dense), domain distribution requirements (each concept should span at least 5-6 different scientific domains), structural requirements (must contain at least one moment of encountering ambiguous evidence, must reach some conclusion).
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 Contrastive twin generation rules \'97 this is critical. The instruction for Stage 3 needs to specify: preserve all factual claims, preserve narrative structure and length, preserve vocabulary register, change ONLY the epistemic disposition. And then explicit examples of what "change only the disposition" looks like \'97 it's not about adding "I think" or "maybe," it's about how the narrator 
\f3\i processes
\f0\i0  the same evidence.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 Quality filters \'97 what makes a pair bad? If the two passages differ in factual content (bad). If the contrast is only surface-level hedge words (bad). If one passage is noticeably longer (bad). If the "humble" version happens to be more correct (confound \'97 sometimes acceptable but should be balanced across the corpus).
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 One refinement I'd add to your pipeline:
\f0\b0  Before Stage 2, have a randomization step that assigns not just which pole goes first, but also which 
\f3\i layer
\f0\i0  of the concept to emphasize. "Intellectual humility" can manifest as uncertainty about data quality, uncertainty about one's own methodology, uncertainty about generalizability, or deference to conflicting evidence. If all your humility passages emphasize the same sub-facet, your vector will be too narrow. The guidelines should specify sub-facets for each concept and ensure roughly even coverage.
\f1\fs24 \

