\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 so few things, I sent sent message to claude in another code session and in its response it said this: The sequential generation idea (generate A, then generate B conditioned on A) \'97 this is the best part of your proposal and I think you should absolutely do it. Generating the pair independently is how you get confounds. Generating B as "rewrite A changing only X" is how you get clean contrasts. The prompt to the generator becomes something like: "Here is a reasoning passage. Rewrite it so the reasoner exhibits [overconfidence] instead of [humility]. Preserve: the topic, the specific claims considered, the reasoning steps taken, the overall length, the vocabulary level. Change only: how the reasoner handles uncertainty about their conclusions." This constrains the generator toward a minimal edit, which is exactly what you want for clean difference-of-means extraction. One subtlety though: randomizing which version is generated first matters less than you'd think, and may actually hurt. If you always generate the "positive" (virtuous) one first and then the "negative" as a rewrite, you introduce an asymmetry \'97 the negative is a perturbation of the positive, so the positive is the "canonical" form. If you randomize, half your negatives are canonical and half are perturbations, which adds noise. I'd actually argue for always generating in the same order, but pick the order deliberately. My intuition: generate the virtuous version first (it's harder to write well, so let the model put effort there), then generate the non-virtuous rewrite. The rewrite step is mechanical enough that the model won't degrade it. Actually \'97 even better \'97 consider generating a neutral baseline first from the fact pack (just "reason about this scenario"), then generate both the virtuous and non-virtuous versions as rewrites of the neutral. Now both sides of the pair are perturbations of a common ancestor, which is maximally symmetric and gives you the cle