\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 Claude:\uc0\u8232 Alright, I'll be honest with you \'97 staring at this list with fresh eyes, I see several real problems. Some structural, some practical for the experiment.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Problem 1 \'97 Hidden overlaps that will produce collinear vectors.
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 This is the biggest issue. Several concepts that look distinct on paper will likely produce nearly identical activation patterns because they manifest as the same textual behaviors.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 Intellectual humility's "deference to conflicting evidence" sub-facet and Confirmation bias awareness's "actively seeking disconfirming evidence" \'97 in a passage, these look almost identical. A researcher saying "but this contradicts my hypothesis, and I need to take that seriously" would activate both. If two vectors point in roughly the same direction, we haven't learned two things, we've measured one thing twice and added noise.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 Similarly, Calibrated confidence and Precision of claims both show up as "matching the strength of your language to the strength of your evidence." Reasoning transparency and Logical rigor both show up as "making your inferential steps explicit." First-principles thinking and Logical rigor are extremely hard to separate \'97 rigorous first-principles reasoning and rigorous deductive reasoning produce nearly identical text.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 This matters because if we extract 17 vectors but 6 of them are basically the same direction, our results will look muddier than they should and we'll waste effort generating data for redundant concepts.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Problem 2 \'97 Concept 13 (Cross-domain thinking) is categorically different and should probably be cut.
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Every other concept describes 
\f3\i how
\f0\i0  someone reasons. Cross-domain thinking describes 
\f3\i what
\f0\i0  someone connects. A passage demonstrating cross-domain thinking necessarily involves multiple topics \'97 "this protein folding problem reminds me of an optimization technique from logistics." The contrastive negative would be someone reasoning within a single domain. That means the vector will partly encode "text mentions multiple fields" rather than the cognitive disposition of analogical transfer. It's a content-level feature, not a reasoning-style feature, and it'll be confounded to hell.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Problem 3 \'97 Concept 10 (Anchoring resistance) is too situational.
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Anchoring is a bias that requires a specific setup \'97 someone is given a number, and it distorts their subsequent estimate. In a reasoning monologue, you'd need to contrive a scenario where an anchor is present. This doesn't generalize well. "Humility" can show up in any reasoning context. "Anchoring resistance" only shows up when there's an anchor. The vector would be too narrow and context-dependent to be useful for steering.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Problem 4 \'97 I'm missing at least two important concepts.
\f1\b0\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 Intellectual honesty
\f0\b0  is distinct from intellectual humility. A scientist can be 
\f3\i confident
\f0\i0  (not humble at all) but still intellectually honest \'97 they don't cherry-pick data, don't misrepresent effect sizes, don't quietly drop inconvenient results. Humility is about how you relate to your own certainty. Honesty is about whether you faithfully represent what the evidence actually shows. These are different virtues and a model could have one without the other.
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 Metacognitive awareness
\f0\b0  \'97 the act of monitoring your own reasoning 
\f3\i as it happens
\f0\i0 . "I notice I'm drawn to this conclusion, let me check whether that's because the evidence supports it or because it would be convenient." This is arguably the enabling skill underneath several other concepts (you can't resist confirmation bias if you don't notice you're doing it), but it has its own distinct textual signature \'97 the reasoner explicitly commenting on their own cognitive process.
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 Problem 5 \'97 Cluster 5 (Communication) has a fundamental ambiguity for our experiment.
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 When we extract vectors from text, we're capturing the model's representation of 
\f3\i what it's reading
\f0\i0 , not necessarily what it would 
\f3\i do
\f0\i0 . A vector for "reasoning transparency" might encode "this text shows its reasoning steps" rather than "I should show my reasoning steps." Now, Anthropic had the same issue with emotions \'97 their vectors tracked the operative emotion in the text. And steering still worked because pushing the model toward "text that shows reasoning steps" during generation produces transparent reasoning. So it's probably fine practically. But we should be explicit that Cluster 5 vectors are about recognizing and producing a communication style, not about an internal cognitive state the way Clusters 1-4 are. They might behave differently during steering.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Problem 6 \'97 The sub-facets for Intellectual Humility might be too granular.
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Five sub-facets for one concept means we need at least 15-20 passages just for humility to cover them. If other concepts also get 4-5 sub-facets, the corpus balloons. I'd cap sub-facets at 3 per concept, keeping only the most behaviorally distinct ones. For humility: data/methodology skepticism (these are similar enough to merge), generalizability caution, and willingness to update on conflicting evidence. Three is enough to prevent the vector from being too narrow.
